I’ll inspect the diffraction pattern, metadata, and the installed crystallography toolchain first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build a GSAS-II refinement, and verify that every submitted artifact is generated from the final project.

The chemistry is constrained to the Pb–W–O system, measured with Cu Kα radiation over an unprocessed reflection scan. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking which installed reference-search/refinement interfaces are usable; the main ambiguity to resolve is whether the product is a single lead tungstate polymorph or a mixture with unreacted/secondary oxides.

The strongest reflections form a coherent scheelite-type PbWO₄ pattern, but they are displaced by roughly +0.25–0.30° 2θ, indicating a specimen-height/zero-offset effect. Several non-scheelite peaks line up with residual PbO and WO₃ families, so I’m testing those polymorphs quantitatively rather than assigning every residual peak by eye.

The peak assignment is becoming more specific: the apparent 47.28° “extra” line is actually PbWO₄ (220), while the 29.35°, 30.62°, 32.75°, and 38.09° set is diagnostic of orthorhombic massicot PbO. A second PbO pattern (litharge) and the low-angle WO₃ triplet are also plausible. I’m comparing nested GSAS-II models now, using fit improvement and physically positive phase scales to decide which are warranted.

The first refinements are running cleanly. I’m deliberately comparing single-addition models before the full mixture: this exposes whether a candidate phase earns its scale independently, rather than allowing several correlated minor phases to trade intensity.

The model sweep is slower than expected because each candidate is being fully re-refined over all 8,282 points, but it is still progressing. No user input is needed; once the nested comparisons finish, I’ll inspect the difference curve for any phase family the database candidates fail to explain.

The four-phase model is statistically better, but its residual is structured, so it is not yet acceptable as a final interpretation. The unexplained lines also resemble mixed-valence lead oxides (especially Pb₃O₄/Pb₂O₃), which are chemically possible near 500 °C. I’m expanding the comparison to those phases; this is preferable to forcing their intensity into PbO and biasing the quantitative fractions.

The mixed-valence comparison is still processing. Early numerical warnings are confined to rejected trial cells during least-squares steps; the projects recover or are discarded, and I will only retain models with stable cells, positive scales, and reproducible convergence.

The sweep has reached the higher-phase-count models, which take longer because the full-cell refinements add many correlated metric terms. I’m letting those complete, but the acceptance criterion remains conservative: a lower profile residual alone will not justify a chemically implausible or unstable phase.

A decisive structural match has emerged: the previously unexplained 25.91°, 29.35°, 30.6–31.05°, 39.54°, and 43.45° reflections follow the C2/m Pb₂MO₅ structure family. The local database contains isostructural Pb₂MoO₅; replacing Mo⁶⁺ with W⁶⁺ gives the chemically expected Pb₂WO₅ phase and predicts the residual peaks as a single coherent pattern. I’m now refining that structural analogue directly rather than retaining the spurious mixed-valence lead-oxide phases.

The Pb₂WO₅ candidate is being tested in all relevant nested combinations: alone with scheelite, then with WO₃ and each PbO polymorph. This will show whether the apparent PbO assignments survive once the correct ternary phase is present, which is essential for unbiased weight fractions.

The higher-order Pb₂WO₅ combinations are numerically heavier because its monoclinic cell contributes four independent metric terms, but the refinements remain active. I’ll retain the smallest phase set that removes distinct unmatched reflections; phases that only marginally lower the residual through overlap will be excluded.

The five-phase model explains the chemically coherent pattern and cuts the profile residual substantially. I’m doing one last “false-positive” audit by adding each plausible oxide polymorph separately; only a phase that produces a stable, meaningful scale and removes its own unique peaks will survive.

The audit is now in the final high-complexity refinements. The main remaining mismatch is line-shape asymmetry and overlapping intensity around 29–31°, not a broad set of unexplained Bragg positions; that points more toward profile/structural-model limitations than another major phase.

The nested audit supports six phases. Pb₂O₃ is not just a statistical surrogate: its two strongest refined reflections fall at 30.14° and 30.28°, matching the otherwise unexplained double feature, and additional lines coincide at ~43.5°, 50.2°, and 52.0°. PbO₂ and Pb₃O₄ alternatives fit less well and lack that full correspondence. I’m keeping Pb₂O₃ and now regularizing the profile/texture model before exporting final quantities.

The final-model refinement is now focused on physically interpretable parameters: axial-divergence asymmetry and heavy-atom coordinates, staged one phase at a time. I’m monitoring coordinate shifts and fraction stability so the improved fit does not come from an implausible structural distortion.

The coordinate refinement is taking longer than the phase-identification runs because each structural derivative is evaluated over the full profile. The project remains stable; if any phase develops unrealistic coordinates or high parameter correlation, I’ll revert that stage and retain the preceding project.

The last heavy-atom stage is still running. This is the final expensive refinement pass; afterward I’ll freeze the accepted model, export CIFs and the exact calculated profile, and validate that all files agree with the saved `.gpx`.

The all-at-once derivative calculation is proving disproportionately slow. I’m allowing this pass a little more time because it is already near completion; if it does not return promptly, I’ll stop it and use the converged pre-coordinate model, whose phase identities and fractions are already stable.

The structural refinements improved the fit without changing the phase set. I’m now replacing the mathematically flexible but partly negative instrumental-width coefficients with a bounded, physically positive profile parameterization. This may raise \(R_{wp}\) slightly, but it gives a more defensible final model and more reliable fractions.

The six-phase refinement has converged (\(R_{wp}\approx11.41\%\), maximum shift/esd 0.24). I’m completing a final trace-phase challenge with Pb₃O₄ and PbO₂; these must improve unique reflections, not merely borrow intensity from the six established phases.

The trace-phase fits are in their final cycles. The accepted six-phase fractions have remained stable across the profile and coordinate tests to roughly 1–2 absolute wt% for major phases and about 0.5–1 wt% for minor phases; the formal covariance errors are smaller than those systematic limits.

The trace-phase challenge is complete. Pb₃O₄ gives only a marginal, highly overlapping ~1 wt% contribution; PbO₂ can lower the residual but all of its strong lines overlap existing phases and it is inconsistent with the 500 °C context. I’m excluding both as non-unique. The final six-phase model is converged and I’m exporting all deliverables directly from that saved project now.

The six-phase refinement is converged. I’m exporting the final GSAS-II project, CIFs, and profile now, then I’ll validate that every reported fraction and fit statistic matches the saved project.

The exports are complete. The selected model is 60.47 wt% PbWO₄, with Pb₂WO₅, WO₃, both PbO polymorphs, and Pb₂O₃ accounting for the remainder; I’m checking CIF readability, fraction normalization, profile arithmetic, and project reloadability before writing the report.

One validation check caught a real export issue in the litharge CIF: the saved atom symmetry/coordinates are being interpreted as Pb₂O in P4/mmm instead of PbO in P4/nmm. I’m correcting that phase model/export before finalizing, while keeping the refined lattice and phase scale tied to the GSAS-II project.

The corrected litharge model both restores PbO stoichiometry and improves the fit materially (Rwp 11.41% → 10.24%); its crystalline fraction rises from the invalid preliminary value to about 4.54 wt%. I’m running the final convergence passes and will regenerate every deliverable from this corrected project.

All six CIFs now parse with the intended formulas and space groups, including corrected litharge PbO, and the profile contains all 8,282 measured points with exact observed-minus-calculated arithmetic. I’m drafting the uncertainty statement around the phase-specific peak evidence and the limits imposed by overlap and the uncalibrated line profile.

The report now records the distinctive peak groups, the exact refinement statistics, and the principal caveats: crystalline-only normalization, microabsorption/overlap, empirical peak shape, and the isostructural basis of Pb₂WO₅. I’m doing one final cross-check against the original CSV and the saved GSAS-II project.

Completed and validated a six-phase GSAS-II refinement:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| PbWO₄, scheelite | 59.43 wt% |
| Pb₂WO₅ | 14.38 wt% |
| WO₃, monoclinic | 9.88 wt% |
| PbO, massicot | 7.08 wt% |
| Pb₂O₃ | 4.68 wt% |
| PbO, litharge | 4.55 wt% |

Final fit: Rwp 10.2345%, Rp 7.3447%, GOF 2.931. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases.

